# tools/al/ales_render_witness.rish -- an ordered chain of real effects over one Clip renders to a canonical RIFF/WAVE .wav,
# atomically, verify-before-trust (resonance-audio).
#
# Chapter: the Six-Chapter double-seat, Chapter C thread (Resonance - the creative suite) - waymark resonance-audio - rung resonance-audio.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-131432_fill-ales217-resonance-render.md
# Idiom kin: resonance/timeline.rye (resonance-audio, the Clip and gain) - resonance/fade.rye - resonance/invert.rye (resonance-audio) - resonance/wav.rye
# (resonance-audio, the container). This rung joins the edit half and the container half into one pipeline endpoint: bytes -> an
# ordered chain of the tree's own proven effects -> the open .wav another program plays.
#
# resonance/render.rye runs a bounded, ordered chain (gain - fade - invert) over a Clip and encodes the result into the canonical
# mono RIFF/WAVE layout. The selftest proves: the render decodes byte-for-byte to the same chain applied by hand (the render
# law); the same steps reordered render to a different file (the order law); a bare chain renders the clip unchanged; a faulty
# step -- a span past the clip, a zero denominator, an output buffer too small, a chain past max_steps, a wild rate -- refuses BY
# NAME before a sample moves, leaving the clip byte-for-byte unchanged (the atomic promise); an empty clip renders a valid
# 44-byte header. A byte container only -- no real file opened, no DAC, no acoustic or electrical fact, no network, no keys, no
# funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_render_witness.rish

say "ales-render: Language EN — resonance-audio joins two proven halves, the editable Clip and the RIFF/WAVE container, into one call: an ordered chain of the tree's own effects becomes a real .wav another program plays."
say "ales-render: Style Radiant — the render law (byte-for-byte to a hand-applied chain), the order law (a sequence not a set), and the atomic promise (a faulty chain is a no-op) each named and proven, every fault refusing by name before a sample moves."
say "ales-render: Lens TAME — the clip's length is invariant under gain, fade, and invert, which is what makes the whole request checkable before it runs; a faulty step leaves the clip byte-for-byte unchanged and nothing written; the sample rate is a carried field not a real clock; siloed to resonance/, no real file, no DAC, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
# THE BINARY LANDS IN A PEN, NEVER A SHARED PATH IN THE TREE (REDS %700). A fixed path is
# one name every run in this checkout writes: one run EXECUTES it while another's linker
# opens it, and the linker refuses with ETXTBSY -- so the guard answers differently on
# bytes nobody changed. `printf %s` trims the newline a bare capture would keep.
let pen = run ["sh" "-c" "printf %s $(mktemp -d)"]
assert pen.ok else "could not make a build pen: ${pen.err_brief}"
let bin = "${pen.out}/render"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p resonance/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} sh tools/fixtures/r/rye_build.sh resonance/render.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-render: build failed — ${build.err_brief}"

let outfile = "resonance/bin/render.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-render: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-render: ${shown.out_brief}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-render' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-render: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-render line"

say "ales-render: GREEN — an ordered chain of proven effects over one Clip renders to a canonical .wav byte-for-byte and atomically; the next Resonance rung can carry a stereo render (the chain over two channels into stereo_wav) or a named preset chain a keeper loads by name."

# The pen is swept here rather than left for the next run to meet.
let pen_swept = run ["sh" "-c" "rm -rf ${pen.out}"]
assert pen_swept.ok else "the build pen outlived its witness: ${pen_swept.err_brief}"
